WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.089
Ransomware demonstrates how an enterprise cyber incident can become an operational crisis.

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:12.179
An attacker does not need to reprogram a controller to stop production.

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:22.328
Loss of identity services, virtualization, engineering workstations, scheduling, quality systems, or operator support applications may make continued operation unsafe or impractical.

00:00:22.328 --> 00:00:31.575
Organizations sometimes shut down proactively because they cannot confirm where the intrusion reached or whether process visibility remains trustworthy.

00:00:31.575 --> 00:00:36.311
That decision protects operations, but it also creates downtime.

00:00:36.311 --> 00:00:46.460
The distinction between business systems and control systems still matters for investigation, yet the recovery plan must account for their dependencies.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.746
OT recovery is more complex than restoring files.

00:00:50.746 --> 00:01:01.797
Teams may need to rebuild trusted workstations, validate controller logic and configurations, confirm network boundaries, reconcile physical process state, and sequence equipment startup.

00:01:01.797 --> 00:01:05.180
Vendors and engineers may be required.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:11.720
A recovered server is not proof that the operation is safe to resume.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:18.712
Segmentation reduces the ability of ransomware to spread from user networks into site operations.

00:01:18.712 --> 00:01:22.095
Controlled identity and administration limit privilege.

00:01:22.095 --> 00:01:30.440
Reliable backups should include controller programs, device configurations, HMI and SCADA applications, historians, servers, and essential documentation.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:34.274
Backups need protected storage and practiced restoration.

00:01:34.274 --> 00:01:43.070
Response planning should define who can isolate networks, pause production, validate equipment, communicate with partners, and authorize restart.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:50.062
Monitor for activity crossing IT and OT boundaries, but interpret alerts with operational context.

00:01:50.062 --> 00:02:00.662
Resilience comes from architecture, preparation, and coordinated recovery, not from assuming that ransomware will remain on one side of an organizational chart.
